Native 120 FPS for SCUS-94154 (experimental): the open 60 FPS gate at twice
the field rate.

PSX_CRASH2_60FPS=1 PSX_CRASH2_FPS=120 [PSX_CRASH2_120FPS_CPU_PCT=100..400,
default 400]. Debug server: crash2_60fps {"fps":120, "cpu_percent_120":400}.

interrupts.c: the VBlank period is a variable. interrupts_set_vblank_divisor(2)
fires VBlank every 282,240 cycles instead of 564,480; nothing else moves - the
root counters, CD, SPU, SIO and DMA keep their device timing, so guest time and
the engine's 1033.6 Hz tick stay where they were. psx_set_vblank_edge_hook()
runs a callback at the end of every VBlank edge. A boot/rematch resets to NTSC.

crash2_60fps.h, with the gate open and 120 asked for:
  - VBlank at 120 Hz while the 3D loop turns.
  - Frame time. func_80016F04 rounds any frame under 19 ticks up to 17, so a
    120 Hz field (8.62 ticks) would be simulated as a 60 Hz one - double
    speed. At the top of the next loop, before any reader, [DB_CUR]+20 is
    replaced by the frame's length in fields (from the game's own stamps,
    [DB_NEXT]+12 - [DB_CUR]+12, nearest field) times 8.5 ticks through a
    half-tick carry: 8 and 9 alternately, 17 a pair - the 60 Hz timeline
    exactly. 2 and 4 fields give 17 and 34 as the quantizer would; 3 and 5,
    which it would round up, 25/26 and 42/43; 53+ ticks keep the game's value.
  - Music. On NTSC libsnd runs its VSync tick mode (SsSetTickMode(1) at
    0x80033D20 becomes mode 5) and SsStart(1) chains the sequencer tick behind
    libetc's VBlank handler: 0x80054E74 calls the displaced handler, then
    [0x8005EFE0]. The VBlank edge hook alternates that pointer between the tick
    (0x80054F04) and an empty function nothing references (0x80016EFC), so the
    tick keeps 59.94 Hz. The SsStart2 form (a VSync callback slot) is handled
    the same way. The stand-in is put back whenever 120 is not engaged,
    including in RAM a savestate brings back.
  - Loads. VSync(n) counts fields and the game waits whole numbers of them
    only outside the loop (VSync 5/20/60). 12 fields with no loop drop VBlank
    to NTSC from the edge hook itself; 4 quick loops re-arm.
  - Scripts: unchanged. The 0036 pacing accumulates ticks, so at 8/9 a field
    it steps every fourth field.
  - Clock. Twice the frames in the same guest second need twice the headroom:
    the gate opens at 400% with VSync's iteration-counted timeout widened x4
    (sll a1,a0,2 / addiu a1,zero,4 - the multiplier must be at least the
    clock over 100). It stays at 400% when 120 steps down to 60: 120 at 400%
    and 60 at 200% give a frame the same instruction budget, so lowering the
    clock would drop a scene that missed its 120 Hz field straight to 30.
  - Staircase. A window that fails at 120 (host behind twice, or frames not
    on one field) steps down to 60 with the gate open - also under Prefer 60,
    which promises 60 - and retries after 10 s, 20 s, 40 s; three in a row
    stay at 60 until a quiet window. Netplay and frame interpolation never
    engage it; demo playback closes the gate as before. The verdict's host
    check counts VBlanks against the rate in force, and a window never spans
    a rate change.
  - Every word it relies on is checked on top of the 60 FPS anchors
    (c2_120_signature_ok), and everything reverts with the gate, on mode off,
    and when the game reloads its executable.

main.cpp: the pacer waits one guest field at the rate in force; driver vsync
never owns cadence while VBlank is faster than NTSC, and the swap interval is
re-derived whenever the rate changes. The speed readout is VBlanks over the
rate in force. psx_frame_interpolation_active() for crash2_60fps.h.
psx_rewind.c: the capture interval is counted in VBlanks, so it scales with the
rate - same seconds of history. freeze_heartbeat.c / debug_server.c:
native_fps_target, native_fps_field_hz, native_120fps_engaged / fallbacks /
stalls / latched; frame_rates speed against the rate in force.

Verified offline with tuning/native120_sim (crash2_60fps.h driven by a model
of the VBlank, root counter 2, the frame end and libsnd's chain, over the real
executable): at 120 the engine is fed 8/9 ticks and 1020 ticks/s - the 60 and
30 Hz timeline - music ticks at 60/s, scripts step at 30/s; stalls drop and
re-arm; a scene over budget at 120 steps down to 60 and holds it; mode off
restores every word, the clock and the VBlank rate. Not verified: how physics
behaves on 8/9-tick steps in play (fixed-point truncation), which is why this
is experimental.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake; no rebuild of generated code.

